Product logos

Every logo in this directory is the trademark of the project or company it
belongs to, and is used only to name that product where it is offered.

Most files come from homarr-labs/dashboard-icons
(https://github.com/homarr-labs/dashboard-icons), licensed under the Apache
License 2.0 — see LICENSE in this directory. Some are renamed after the
product they are used for (seerr.svg is the collection's jellyseerr.svg), the
raster ones — docker-compose.webp among them — were resized to 96px WebP, and
gotify.svg had an empty Illustrator extension element removed. github.svg,
nextjs.svg and tailscale.svg are the collection's -light variants, drawn for a
dark ground; lets-encrypt.svg is the collection's file with its navy #003a70
lifted to #73a8e7, the same hue at the L 0.72 rung, so the padlock's body reads
on a dark ground.

The host's own marks come from the same collection: ubuntu.svg, debian.svg,
arch.svg, alpine.svg, rocky.svg, almalinux.svg and linuxmint.svg are its
ubuntu-linux, debian-linux, arch-linux, alpine-linux, rocky-linux, alma-linux
and linux-mint files renamed after the platform name the host reports;
amd.svg is its amd-light variant; arm.svg, qemu.svg, fedora.svg, centos.svg
and opensuse.svg keep their names. debian.svg, fedora.svg, alpine.svg and
centos.svg had their darkest colour lifted to the same hue at the L 0.72 rung
(#a80030 to #ff6a79, #294172 to #87a5dd, #0d597f to #6aadd7, #262577 to
#8e9cf9) so they read on a dark ground.

The marks for what a backup covers and where it goes, and for the programs a
terminal runs, come from the same collection: git.svg, backblaze.svg,
nodejs.svg, python.svg, vim.svg, neovim.svg, npm.svg, kubernetes.svg and
terraform.svg keep their names; claude.svg is its claude-ai.svg and deno.svg
its deno-light variant.

terminal.svg is no product's mark and comes from nowhere: it is drawn for
this project, as the mark of a terminal window whose program has none.

The marks for what signs in to the account pages come from the same
collection: chrome.svg, firefox.svg, safari.svg, opera.svg, brave.svg,
vivaldi.svg, android.svg, gitlab.svg, jenkins.svg, ansible.svg,
home-assistant.svg and postman.svg keep their names; edge.svg is its
microsoft-edge.svg, windows.svg its windows-11.svg, apple.svg its apple-light
variant, drawn for a dark ground, and linux.svg its tux.svg.

curl.svg is from Simple Icons (CC0 1.0), filled with curl's #073551 lifted to
#7eaacc, the same hue at the L 0.72 rung, because Simple Icons ships the path
alone and the navy disappears on a dark ground.

bun.svg is from Simple Icons (CC0 1.0), filled with Bun's cream #fbf0df
because Simple Icons ships the path alone and its brand hex is black.

jupyter.svg is from Simple Icons (https://simpleicons.org), CC0 1.0, and so
are intel.svg and pm2.svg, filled with their brand colour lifted to the L 0.72
rung (#0071C5 to #4fa9ff, #2B037A to #9a93ff) because Simple Icons ships the
path alone.

